  "full_response": "Object-oriented programming (OOP) is a programming paradigm that uses \"objects\" to design software. It is centered around objects, which are instances of classes. A class consists of attributes (fields) and methods (functions) that the behavior and properties of the objects. The key principles of OOP are encapsulation, inheritance, abstraction, and polymorphism. Here's a overview of each, along with examples in Python:\n\n1. **Encapsulation**: Encapsulation is the bundling of data (attributes) and methods (functions) that operate on the data into a single unit or class. It restricts direct access to some of the object's components, which is a means of preventing accidental interference and misuse of the ways objects are intended to be used.\n\n   Example:\n   ```python\n   class Person:\n       def __init__(, name, age):\n           self._name = name  # Note the underscore prefix by convention\n           self._age = age    # Note the underscore prefix by convention\n\n       def get_name(self):\n           return self._name\n\n       def get_age(self):\n           return self._age\n\n       def set_age(self, age):\n           if age > 0:\n               self._age = age\n   ```\n\n2. **Inheritance**: Inheritance is a way to form new classes using classes that have already been defined. The new class, known as a derived class, inherits attributes and methods of the existing class, which is known as the base class. This promotes code reusability.\n\n   Example:\n   ```python\n   class Vehicle:\n       def __init__(self, brand, model):\n           self.brand = brand\n           self.model = model\n\n       def display_info(self):\n           print(f\"This is a {self.brand} {self.model}.\")\n\n   class Car(Vehicle):  # Inherits Vehicle class\n       def __init__(self, brand, model, horsepower):\n           super().__init__(brand, model)  # Call the superclass constructor\n           self.horsepower = horsepower\n\n       def display_horsepower(self):\n           print(f\"This car has {self.horsepower} horsepower.\")\n   ```\n\n3. **Abstraction**: Abstraction means hiding the complex reality while exposing only the necessary parts. In OOP, abstraction allows us to focus on what an object does instead how it does it. It is achieved by creating abstract classes and interfaces.\n\n   Example:\n   ```python\n   from abc import ABC, abstractmethod\n\n   class",
